-- Q3170: Advanced Consolidations [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 810-10-40-5, when a parent loses control of a subsidiary, it measures any retained interest at fair value, adds the proceeds received, and recognizes a gain or loss equal to the difference between that total and the carrying amount of the subsidiary''s net assets including goodwill and NCI.
Wrong (A): Consolidation ceases on the date control is lost; the subsidiary is not consolidated through year-end once the parent no longer controls it.
Wrong (B): A disposal of a subsidiary interest is not a stock split; it is a deconsolidation event requiring gain or loss recognition under ASC 810.
Wrong (C): Writing off the investment as an impairment charge is incorrect; the parent must compute a gain or loss using proceeds and fair value of any retained interest.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3170;

-- Q3174: Advanced Consolidations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 810-10-10-1, consolidated financial statements present the parent and subsidiaries as a single economic entity, requiring elimination of all intercompany transactions. The management fee revenue at the parent and corresponding expense at the subsidiary cancel because they are internal transfers with no economic substance to the consolidated group.
Wrong (B): Reporting the fee as revenue and expense on separate lines would overstate both consolidated revenue and expenses, violating the single-entity concept.
Wrong (C): Reporting only the expense side would create an imbalance and misstate consolidated results; both sides of the intercompany transaction must be removed.
Wrong (D): Deferral applies to timing differences, not intercompany eliminations; the fee must be eliminated entirely regardless of payment status.$EXPL$
